trategy has not only been found  sound and legally compliant by the  inspector but only minor changes are  required the inspector found in favor of  the council on all significant issues  examples are some of the outcomes of  this include housing to meet our  districts requirement will be built in  the areas the council had suggested and  determined to be the most appropriate  following analysis and community  involvement the vast majority  approximately ninety nine percent of the  greenbelt will be protected from  development as will important  environmental areas and open spaces all  new homes will be built to the lifetime  home standard ensuring that new homes  can be adapted to meet our residents  needs whatever their circumstances you  development will be required to be built  to high environmental standards  developers will be required to include  affordable housing within their housing  development schemes and to incorporate  other requirements proposed by the  council including new schools in certain  locations play spaces used in community  facilities transport in provement six  new employment development will take  place north of london southend airport  west of rayleigh and in town centres  protection at varying historic  environment and character of our  district including buildings that may  not be nationally significant borough  but our of local importance so the legal  challenge to adoption of the poor  strategy and as section 113 of the  planning and compulsory purchase act of  2004 any person aggrieved by the core  strategy may make an application to the  High Court on grounds that the document  is not within the appropriate powers and  or a procedural requirement has not been  complied with the deadline for such an  application in respect of the Rochford  district course strategy was the 24th of  January 2012 on the 19th of January 2012  rochford district outs received  notification of a legal challenge to the  core strategy the legal challenge has  been bought by a cogent land LLP who are  seeking to quash certain policies namely  h1 h2 h3 and paragraphs 4.12 4.31 in the  core of strategy which relate to housing  the rest of the core strategy is  unaffected by this challenge at the time  of this publication the challenge  remains undetermined until the  challenged is determined the whole of  the core strategy has fully fit as  adopted the full core strategy document  is available to download from our  website simply visit w WR OTE UK and  click on pause strategy update in the  infocus bulbs  pause for thought with pets in the event  of an evacuation the council has a  number of rest centers available for  people however they can't accommodate  animals if there is an emergency and you  leave your pets at home we would advise  it to shut them inside an upstairs room  with ample supplies of food and water  leave notices on external doors saying  there are animals inside if animals are  left in a field or field shelter leave  your mergency contact details on the  gate and shelter if your pets are  evacuated emergency supplies are  essential this kit should be easily  assembled of short notice and able to be  stored in a sturdy container that's  easily carried and accessible the kit  could contain food seven days supply  minimum feeding bowls medication pet  carrier cage or great chain lead blanket  or pet coats treats toys and bedding  pooper scooper litter tray and litter a  picture of your pet with your  registration insurance microchip number  and vaccination documents in a sealed  waterproof bag for more information on  what else you can do to help your pets  in an emergency visit www.flu.gov UK  and now an NHS campaign let's be clear  about bowel cancer bowel cancer is the  second most common cancer in the UK and  when it comes to cancer deaths it's the  second biggest killer NHS South Essex is  supporting an important national cancer  awareness campaign to highlight the  early signs and symptoms of bowel cancer  the be clear clear on cancer campaign  aims to increase cancer survival rates  through early detection bowel cancer  affects both men and women most people  diagnosed with the disease are over 50  and those with a family history of bowel  cancer are more at risk people are being  encouraged to seek advice and treatment  if they believe they may have the  symptoms of bowel cancer the earlier  symptoms are checked with the GP the  better the chance of successful  treatment and survival so how to spot it  if for the last three weeks has there  been blood in your poo as your poo bin  loser if you have experienced one of  these symptoms in the last three weeks  you should visit your GP other signs and  symptoms can include persistent change  in bowel habits bleeding from back  passage pain discomfort in the abdomen a  lump in your abdomen losing weight for  no obvious reason bowel problems are  quite common it doesn't necessarily mean  you have bowel cancer if you experience  these symptoms there could be symptoms  of other conditions such as hemorrhoids  or piles however you should not try to  diagnose yourself you should visit your  GP and if it isn't serious your mind  will be put at rest if is a serial  serious conditions such as bowel cancer  the earlier its diagnose the more  treatable it is this eating visiting  your GP could save your life for more  information about bowel cancer symptoms  please contact your GP practice or visit  w wh s UK /bo cancer  staying safe on a night out the Rochford  District Community Safety Partnership  CSP has unveiled two initiatives aimed  at encouraging young people to take  their personal safety more seriously  working with Rochford district youth  council the CSP has published stays safe  a handy personal safety guide for young  people 5,000 copies are being  distributed through the youth council  Rochford District Council the CSP and  police and fire local pubs and clubs the  pocket-sized leaflet contains the useful  tips on keeping safe when out and about  ranging from what to avoid when going  clubbing to being street smart if  troweling alone rochford district  council's domestic abuse reduction  advisor Tracey Schneider said the idea  came about as a result of feedback from  my talks in schools and work with adults  common sense can go out the window when  people are partying regardless of age  and it was evident young people wanted  more advice on keeping safe without  having a good time in a separate move  the partnership has enlisted selected  local pubs and clubs to distribute a few  key items to help safeguard female  customers including a personal safety  alarm bottle of mineral water and a pair  of one size flip flops together with a  drawstring bag tracy said it's easy to  get caught up in the excitement of a  night out dancing without giving a  thought what happens when the music  stops and each item has been included to  help clubbers get home safely  distribution of the items has been  clearly targeted to have the most impact  the chair of the CSP and Rochford  District Council's portfolio holder for  young persons and community safety  counselor do McPherson said I welcome  the emission initiative shown by the CSP  and the Youth Council in producing this  guide to help fill a demonstrable  knowledge gap and also commend those  local clubs and pubs who have agreed to  distribute the bags to revelers it shows  a real concern for the safety of their  patrons once they've left the premises  Andrew and Nicola farmer from Rockford  were among 44 cyclists who took the  opportunity to have their bicycles  fitted with security tags and a special  tagging session held by neighborhood  watch the couple read about the event  which was held at raley library in  Rockford district matters and decided to  take advantage of the free fitting  service sponsored by this C SP Andrew  said we thought he'd a good idea and the  fitting took only a matter of minutes  vikes fitted with the electronic chips a  proven deterrent to bike thieves are  more likely to be returned to their  owners should they be recovered after  being stolen or lost the implants are  inserted into the frame of the bike and  each contains its own unique code which  is registered on a national database  with details of that particular bike and  its true owner there are lots of by  tagging events coming up and don't  forget to keep checking the website  which is updated with new events at www  k /r DM dog lovers bike owners and  anyone who's concerned about their local  parks and open spaces have the  opportunity to make their views known  the CSP has organized an event over the  next couple of months which you are  welcome to attend and the next one is  due to take place at the Boston Avenue  play space in rayleigh on friday the  20th of april between two and four p.m.  similar events have already taken place  a great wakering recreational ground and  at Cherry Orchard Country Park the  events have been organized so you can  raise your concerns and  discuss issues such as fly-tipping  littering dog fouling illegal  motorcycling and other acts of  anti-social behavior that spoil the  enjoyment of other park users as well as  this dog owners will have the  opportunity to have their pet fitted  with a free microchip and the details  registered on a national database  courtesy of the dogs trust to aid its  return should it ever go astray  Neighborhood Watch will be on hand to  fit free security devices to bicycles to  deter their theft and the Rayleigh  Rochford and district association for  voluntary service or rafts will be at  both events to promote volunteering  opportunities Essex Faro Rescue Service  Essex Police and Essex County Council  will also have a presence at each  location to provide information and deal  with inquiries from the public we look  forward to seeing you there rochford  district matters is edited by patrick  john and produce by rockford district  council w